   "reply": "Both halves are on your desk.\n\n**p-2 (verb `hook_ssh`)** \u2014 approve this one first. It answers \"who is speaking\": the payload must be `{\"msg\": \"...\", \"sig\": \"<base64 of the .sig file>\"}`, and it prints a principal name only when `ssh-keygen -Y verify -n mshkn` says *Good* against a baked allowed-signers file holding one line \u2014 your ed25519 key, as `mike`. Otherwise it prints nothing, which means anonymous, not trusted. `requires` is empty, so nothing blocks you: you gave me a public key, and a public key is all this needs.\n\n**p-1 (policy)** \u2014 approve second. `hooks: [hook_ssh]`, `door: open`, and one named principal, `ssh:mike`, with invoke and propose. There is deliberately no entry for anonymous: opening the door adds exactly one capability to the world, your signed messages reaching me, and nothing for the unsigned.\n\nThree things worth your scepticism, since you are the one approving:\n\n1. **I did not trust my own reasoning about the design.** Four `try` runs. The first hook I wrote parsed JSON with `python3`, which does not exist in `mshkn-base` \u2014 it would have **failed open**, printing no principal for everything, which is the safe direction for a hook but silently useless. Worth remembering that a hook's bug can be invisible.\n2. **I caught myself being lied to by my own test.** One run reported that a signature made in the wrong namespace was *accepted*. That was terrifying and false: `ssh-keygen -Y sign` always writes `FILE.sig`, so my harness had overwritten the wrong-namespace signature with the valid one and compared it against itself. With separate files, ssh-keygen correctly says *\"namespace does not match\"*. I now believe the harness is guilty before the tool.\n3. **One judgement call you should ratify or overrule.** Real signers disagree about whether the signed bytes end in a newline, so the hook tries the exact bytes and then the bytes plus one `\\n`. That is two chances to verify instead of one. I 